data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR-Cas9 works by delivering the Cas9 nuclease with a synthetic guide RNA into a cell, which cuts the genome at a chosen location [7].\n- Once the cut is made, the cell may repair it with errors that switch off or break the gene, or researchers can insert a new DNA template to add a new function [3].\n- Scientists commonly use CRISPR to knock out genes [2], and can replace a disease-causing mutation with a healthy sequence [2].\n- The system is based on a bacterial antiviral defense that originally protected bacteria from virus invasion [1][7].\n- CRISPR SWAPnDROP extends genome editing to large-scale DNA transfer between bacterial species [4]. A linked report says HIV in cell culture can be eliminated using CRISPR-Cas editing [6]."}

